{"v":1,"groups":{"neon":{"n":"Neon-type tetras","tip":"Cardinal: the red stripe runs the whole length of the belly, from the gills to the tail. Neon: the red covers only the rear half of the body. Green neon: much smaller, with a short red patch near the tail.","h":1},"rummy":{"n":"Rummy-nose tetras","tip":"True rummy-nose (Hemigrammus): red on the nose only, with clean black-and-white tail bars. Brilliant/false rummy (Petitella): the red covers more of the head and the tail bars are bolder.","h":1},"bigtetra":{"n":"Large silvery tetras","tip":"Congo: big scales with a rainbow sheen, males have flowing fins. Emperor: dark stripe along the body, with a longer extended tail middle. Buenos Aires and black skirt tetras are deeper-bodied with dark bands."},"skirt":{"n":"Skirt tetras","tip":"Black skirt: wide black fade at the rear and tall fins. Serpae: red-orange body with a black shoulder spot. Bleeding heart: a single red spot behind the gill on a pink body."},"redbarb":{"n":"Barbs","tip":"Rosy: copper-pink body with a black tail base spot in males that glow rosy red. Cherry: red body with a dark stripe through the eye. Tiger: four black vertical bars with orange-red fins. Odessa: red stripe along the middle of the body. Gold and checkerboard: golden or spotted."},"algae":{"n":"Algae eaters","tip":"Siamese algae eater: a clean black stripe from the nose through the eye all the way to the end of the tail. Flying fox: a gold stripe above the black one, and the stripe stops at the tail base. Chinese algae eater: golden, larger sucker mouth and a blotchy stripe, grows big and aggressive.","h":1},"cory":{"n":"Corydoras","tip":"Skunk: black stripe along the back with an orange tint. Panda: black eye patches, black tail base and dorsal spot. Bronze/Aeneus: plain metallic bronze or green. Albino: pale pink with red eyes. Julii: spotted head with lines. False julii: more lines. Peppered: dark pepper spots over gray. Sterbai: dark with orange pectoral fins. Pygmy: under 1 inch, swims mid-water.","h":1},"loach":{"n":"Loaches","tip":"Kuhli: thin eel-like with dark and tan bands. Clown: orange with black bands and red fins. Yoyo: marks that read \"Y-O-Y-O\" along the body. Zebra: pale with dark vertical stripes. Dojo/weather loach: long, plain, whiskered. Hillstream: flat, clings to glass."},"rainbow":{"n":"Rainbowfish","tip":"Boesemani: front half blue-gray, back half orange-yellow. Turquoise: blue-green overall. Australian/splendid: stripe along the side with orange fins. Dwarf neon: under 2 inches with a red-stripe fade. Red: all red males. Threadfin: very tall twin dorsal fins.","h":1},"guppy":{"n":"Guppies, Endlers and platies","tip":"Endler: under an inch and a half, bright pattern, small tail. Guppy: larger, big fan-like tail in many colors. Platy: stockier with a rounded tail. Swordtail: males have a long sword on the tail. Molly: larger and taller with a big dorsal fin.","h":1},"danio":{"n":"Danios and white clouds","tip":"Zebra: horizontal blue-white stripes. Pearl: pearly sheen with a pink stripe. Leopard: spots instead of stripes. White cloud: tiny with a red tail base and a gold stripe. Celestial pearl danio: tiny with pale spots on dark blue.","h":1},"rasbora":{"n":"Rasboras","tip":"Harlequin: copper body with a black triangle. Lambchop: thinner, longer black wedge. Espei: red-orange with a black wedge that starts at the middle. Chili: tiny and bright red. Scissortail: black-and-white striped tail.","h":1},"gourami":{"n":"Gouramis","tip":"Dwarf: small, with blue and red stripes. Honey: yellow-orange, small. Pearl: pearly spots with a dark line from nose to tail. Three-spot/blue/gold: larger, with a spot mid-body and one at the tail base. Kissing: pink, with big lips."},"pleco":{"n":"Plecos","tip":"Bristlenose: fleshy tentacles on the snout. Common: very large with a sail-like dorsal fin. Clown pleco: small with orange bands. Zebra pleco: small, bold black-and-white stripes. Otocinclus: tiny with a dark stripe, a flat sucker."},"stick":{"n":"Twig and whiptail catfish","tip":"Twig catfish (Farlowella): very thin like a twig, with a long pointed snout. Whiptail (Rineloricaria): flatter body with a long thin whip-like tail."},"cichmal":{"n":"Malawi cichlids","tip":"Yellow lab: bright yellow with a black dorsal edge. Red zebra: orange to peach with a blotchy pattern. Cobalt zebra: blue with dark bars. Demasoni: small, blue with thick black bars. Kenyi: males yellow-orange, females blue bars. Peacocks: brilliant solid colors, long pointed body.","h":1},"cichsa":{"n":"South American cichlids","tip":"Angelfish: tall with long trailing fins. Oscar: large, with an orange-patterned dark body and a tail spot. Convict: black and white vertical stripes. Jack Dempsey: dark with bright blue-green flecks. Severum: round, with a faint orange or gold tint. Blue acara: gray-blue with a vertical dark bar behind the gill and blue speckles. Firemouth: red throat and belly. Keyhole: tiny with a keyhole-shaped spot."},"dwarfcich":{"n":"Dwarf cichlids","tip":"German blue ram: blue-yellow with a black spot behind the head and red fins. Bolivian ram: duller, with a stripe over the eye. Apistogramma: small with a rounded tail, males show a dark horizontal stripe and a frilled dorsal."},"tanganyika":{"n":"Tanganyika cichlids","tip":"Julidochromis: elongated with black stripes. Princess (brichardi): elegant, with extended fins and a light blue edge. Shell dwellers: tiny, rest in shells. Cyprichromis: slender, free-swimming and in schools."},"puffer":{"n":"Pufferfish","tip":"Pea puffer: tiny yellow-green with brown spots. Figure-8: rounder, with a figure-8 pattern on its back. Both are small, rounded and slow-moving."},"betta":{"n":"Bettas and bubble-nest fish","tip":"Betta males have long flowing fins; females and plakats have short fins. Paradise fish: striped with red and blue and a forked tail."},"goldfish":{"n":"Goldfish and koi","tip":"Common/comet: long body with a single tail. Fantail and Ryukin: rounded body with a double tail. Oranda: has a head hood. Koi: barbels and a wide range of colors."},"shrimpf":{"n":"Freshwater shrimp","tip":"Cherry: bright red (Neocaridina). Crystal red: red and white bands. Amano: gray-clear with dotted lateral spots. Ghost/glass: fully transparent. Bamboo/wood: large and brown, with fan arms. Blue velvet: solid blue."},"nerite":{"n":"Nerite and mystery snails","tip":"Zebra nerite: black stripes on gold. Tiger nerite: bold dark stripes. Horned nerite: has little horns. Olive nerite: plain, with a speckled olive shell. Mystery/apple snail: large, round, smooth shell in gold, blue or ivory."},"cfish":{"n":"Clownfish","tip":"Ocellaris: orange with three white bands and thin black edging. Percula: thicker black edging between bands and more rounded. Maroon: dark red with a single bright stripe. Clarkii: darker body with a yellow tail. Tomato: bright red-orange, with a single patch behind the eye.","h":1},"tang":{"n":"Tangs","tip":"Yellow: all bright yellow. Regal/blue/hippo: blue with a black palette shape and yellow tail. Purple: purple with a yellow tail. Kole: dark with a gold ring around the eye. Sailfin: tall with a large dorsal sail. Powder blue: sky blue with a white face and yellow dorsal."},"damsel":{"n":"Damsels and chromis","tip":"Blue-green chromis: bright blue-green, shoals in groups. Yellowtail damsel: royal blue with a yellow tail. Domino: black with white spots. Three-stripe: white with three black bars. Azure: blue and yellow.","h":1},"gobyb":{"n":"Gobies and blennies","tip":"Firefish: white nose and a red tail with a long dorsal spike. Yellow watchman: yellow body with a long dorsal fin. Lawnmower blenny: brown with a sloped face. Tailspot blenny: spotted with a stripe on the tail. Bicolor blenny: two-tone, with a dark head and yellow tail.","h":1},"mandarin":{"n":"Mandarin dragonets","tip":"Mandarin: orange, blue and green swirls, slow along the rock. Psychedelic mandarin: the same but with a more intense maze-like pattern."},"wrasse":{"n":"Wrasses and other reef fish","tip":"Six-line wrasse: six stripes. Melanurus: a black tail spot. Fairy wrasse: bright with long fins. Cleaner wrasse: thin blue stripe.","h":1},"angel":{"n":"Angelfish and butterflyfish","tip":"Flame angel: red-orange with vertical dark bars. Coral beauty: blue to orange with a dark shoulder. Copperband butterfly: long snout with a copper-colored stripe."},"euph":{"n":"Euphyllia corals","tip":"Hammer: heads with hammer-shaped or anchor tentacle tips. Frogspawn: branching tentacle tips like frog eggs. Torch: long single-tipped tentacles. Duncan: short, round, fleshy polyps on a small stony skeleton.","h":1},"softc":{"n":"Soft corals","tip":"Xenia: pulsing, waving stalks. Green star polyps: bright green mats with small arms. Kenya tree: a tree-like stalk branching into small heads. Leather (toadstool): wide, thick cap that folds in. Clove polyps: dense stalks with pale tips.","h":1},"zoa":{"n":"Zoanthids and mushrooms","tip":"Zoanthid/palythoa: groups of small round polyps in colonies and mats. Mushrooms (Discosoma): flat disc, no stalk. Ricordea: flatter bumpy disks. Rhodactis: larger and hairy-looking.","h":1},"sps":{"n":"SPS corals","tip":"Acropora: branching with a tip polyp. Montipora: plating or encrusting with tiny polyps. Birdsnest/Seriatopora: thin fine branches. Stylophora: thick branches.","h":1},"lps":{"n":"LPS corals","tip":"Brain (Lobophyllia/Favia): round or dome-shaped with ridged, maze-like valleys. Trachyphyllia: flat and folded. Goniopora: long flowerpot-like polyps, delicate. Bubble coral: bubbly sacs. Candy cane: each polyp on its own stalk.","h":1},"anemone":{"n":"Anemones","tip":"Bubble-tip: bulb tips on tentacles. Long tentacle: long flowing tentacles and a bigger body. Carpet: flat, wide, sticky tentacles. Condy: long, thin tentacles with pink tips. Aiptasia: tiny glassy pest.","h":1},"crab":{"n":"Hermit crabs and reef crabs","tip":"Blue-leg hermit: blue legs with orange bands. Red-leg hermit: red legs. Emerald crab: green, mostly stationary. Sally Lightfoot: bright red and orange, runs fast."},"reefsnail":{"n":"Reef snails","tip":"Turbo: large and round, with a rough shell. Nassarius: small cones that burrow. Astraea: flat coin-like shell. Cerith: thin spiral."},"sword":{"n":"Sword plants","tip":"Amazon sword (Echinodorus): large, wide leaves growing from a crown. Narrow-leaf swords: thinner leaves. Often large and stand in the middle or back of the tank."},"crypt":{"n":"Cryptocoryne","tip":"Crypts form a rosette with crinkled leaves. Wendtii: wavy leaves in green to bronze. Parva: tiny and compact. Balansae: long, strap-like leaves."},"val":{"n":"Vallisneria and sagittaria","tip":"Vallisneria: long, thin ribbon leaves. Sagittaria: grass-like blades on a base, usually shorter. Jungle val: giant and broad."},"stem":{"n":"Stem plants","tip":"Ludwigia: oval leaves in pairs on a stem, red underside. Rotala: tiny narrow leaves in whorls, pink to red tips. Bacopa: thick round leaves. Hygrophila: pointy, often broad leaves. Alternanthera: red or maroon leaves."},"fern":{"n":"Java fern, anubias and mosses","tip":"Java fern: finger-like fronds, small black spots under leaves. Anubias: thick dark arrowhead leaves. Mosses: green fuzzy mats on wood or rocks. Bolbitis: wavy dark fronds."},"float":{"n":"Floating plants","tip":"Frogbit: small round leaves with roots hanging down. Water lettuce: rosettes like tiny cabbages. Salvinia: round, hairy leaves. Duckweed: tiny dots covering the surface."},"carpet":{"n":"Carpet plants","tip":"Dwarf hairgrass: very fine grass-like carpet. Monte Carlo: rounded tiny leaves. Dwarf baby tears: tiny round leaves in a dense mat. Glosso: tiny paddle leaves."},"macro":{"n":"Macroalgae","tip":"Chaeto: fuzzy green balls or strands. Caulerpa: grape-like or feathery green runners. Halimeda: segmented, stiff."}},"species":[{"n":"Acan Coral","s":"Acanthastrea lordhowensis","k":"coral","w":"s","a":["acan","acanthastrea","acanthophyllia"],"g":"lps"},{